<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- MainWindow — the app shell. The map fills the whole window; the UI is ONE bar overlaid on its bottom
     edge, plus a small hideable NOTCH at the top of each map pane.

       ┌──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┐
       │       ┌──────┴──────┐       │       ┌──────┴──────┐       │  pane NOTCHES — top-centre of each
       │       │Ref˅│▓▒░▒▓│0.5°˅     │       │Vel˅│▓▒░▒▓│0.5°˅     │  pane: product, colour-ramp legend,
       │       └──╮ ˄ Hide ╭──┘      │       └──╮ ˄ Hide ╭──┘      │  tilt. Quad shown; single/two
       │        map pane 2           │        map pane 3           │  collapse to fewer cells. The tab
       ├─────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤  hides one notch, independently.
       │       ┌─────────────┐       │       ┌─────────────┐       │
       │       │SRV˅│▓▒░▒▓│0.5°˅     │       │CC ˅│▓▒░▒▓│0.5°˅     │
       │       └──╮ ˄ Hide ╭──┘      │       └──╮ ˄ Hide ╭──┘      │
       │    map pane 0  (MAIN)       │        map pane 1           │
       ├─────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┤
       │  ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  │  …thrown UP onto the map
       │                 ╭ ˅ Tools ╮ ╭ ˅ Bar ╮                     │  ONE rail, one tab per tier
       │ [Search          ✕⌕] ▣ ⊚    ╎◗✈⚗ │ ⌕ │ ⌸◎ ┊┊┊ [⌂ KTLX Oklahoma City ˅]  ▢▥▦ ┊┊ ⌘⛶ │ ▥▴ ┇[Isolate ▾]│  TOOLS tier
       │  ▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒╎▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒┆▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒┇▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒│  ╎ = console tools' left = console's
       │                              ╎1:07 PM ═══ scrubber ═══ VCP:     212┆              ┇          │  ┆ = picker's right edge = scan text's
       │ ⋮ ((•))│⋮   ☁ │⋮             ╎        3/10  ◀ ▶ ▶  ↺  Mode:  precip │              ┇📡   ⚙  –│□│  ┇ = Isolate's left edge = ATLAS's
       │ PastCast NowCast ForeCast    ╎2 min ago                SAILS/MRLE:×1│  (activity)  ┇Atlas Settings ✕│  ┊┊┊/┊┊ = the STRETCHING separators
       └──────────────────────────────┴───────────── ↑ bar midpoint ─────────┴──────────────┴──────────┘
          ↑ NO RULES ANYWHERE — every seam is a soft SHADOW instead, and each one
            is drawn by whichever side owns it: the tools tier throws its own up
            onto the map, and the BAR draws the band it receives from the tier
            above it. Same treatment on all four pane notches, on three sides.
            OverlayBar.EdgeStyle picks per instance, because only the host knows
            what is beyond a given edge. ⚠️ The bar's value MOVES at runtime: hide
            the tools tier and the bar is suddenly the top of the stack, so it
            starts casting instead of receiving — and both tabs drop to the bar's own
            face at the same moment, because a tab wears the plate it stands on.
            Both facts are MainWindow.ApplyRailSeating's: one rule, one method.
          ↑ TWO TIERS, ONE RAIL. Both tabs wear the plate they STAND ON, not the
            tier they toggle, so they move together with the stack. They are
            named because that is what tells them apart — two lozenges both
            reading "Hide" name no difference. The arrow points the way the tier moves.
            The rail is shared because it must be: an OverlayBar draws its tab
            in row 0 of its own grid, so the lower tier's tab would land in the
            seam between the two. Both tiers run ShowTab=False.
          ↑ THE CONSOLE ⇄ TEMPORAL SWAP (2026-10-06, mock-up A): the time module holds the bar's MIDPOINT and
            the temporal keys sit at the LEFT edge, so the bar reads mode → time → windows. The tools tier
            follows: its centre section spans the console's edges with the site picker on ▶ — the bar's
            midpoint (AlignCentreSection) — and
            the place search + ▣ ⊚ moved to the left, over the temporal keys.
          ↑ Site markers AND ⌕ Inspect are on the TOOLS tier (both left this bar; see MapControlsStrip).
          ↑ ⋮ = each mode's SIDE CAR: a full-height settings latch on its own key, lit only while that mode
            runs. One window per mode. (Same three-dot mark the retired shared panel key used.)
          ↑ ▢▥▦ = pane layout, three toggles (1 / 2 / 4) on the TOOLS tier; it left this bar's right
            cluster, which is now window latches only. See MapControlsStrip.
          ↑ ▣ = the (future) marker viewer and ⊚ = Location follow the place search on the TOOLS
            tier. Location left this bar: it is a "where on the map" move like search, and an
            occasional one, so it fails the bar's constant-gesture test. See MapControlsStrip.
          ↑ [⌂ KTLX ˅] = the SITE PICKER (home + favorites), on the tools tier over the time module.
            It replaced this bar's Home key AND the side car that used to hang off the Atlas key — two doors
            to one list. The tools tier's left is radar + map (which site), its right is map only.
            See MapControlsStrip. Don't re-add a Home key here.
          ↑ 📡 = Atlas, a plain window latch again, like Settings.
          ↑ The ACTIVITY SLOT, in the bar's empty run between the console and Atlas —
              tools tier:                  ▢▥▦ ┊ ⌘⛶ │ ▥▴ ┇[Isolate ▾]
              bar:   console ┃  ┌┃ Loading the loop · KTLX   22 of 39 ┐┇Atlas
                             ┃  └┃ ████████░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░┘
                                ↑ left = the console's right + 24   ↑ right = Atlas less 8 px (AlignActivityBay)
            Only while Anvil is working (a loop loading without its gate, the site check — which used to be a
            toast over the map); empty bar otherwise. See Composites/BarActivityReadout.
          ↑ Not drawn above: the LOOP HOLDING GATE — while a PastCast loop loads, the whole map (notches and the
            rail's see-through gaps included; the opaque tiers and both tabs paint over it) is frosted behind
            "Loading the loop" + progress bars + Cancel load / Use the map. See Composites/LoopHoldingGate.

     ⚠️ WHAT REPEATS PER PANE IS NOT IN THE BAR ANY MORE. It used to be: the bar's left cluster carried a
     160px "chip" per pane (a combined product-selector-and-colour-ramp) plus the one tilt combo, which is
     what made the bar's left half a width BUDGET that the centred temporal keys had to be defended from.
     Both moved into the pane notches — where the legend sits on the field it describes — so the bar's left
     is now just the global time module and the chip's width floor is gone with it. Add a PER-PANE control
     to PaneNotchContent; add a GLOBAL one to RadarControls or to the right cluster here.

     ⚠️ THE PANE WATERMARK IS GONE TOO (the big ghosted "Ref" in a pane's corner). The notch says the same
     thing better, a few pixels away. See the PANE NOTCHES block below.

     ⚠️ MOST keys in the bar are a SQUARE holding a MARK OVER A NAME. The exception, on purpose, is each
     temporal key (a square mode half PLUS a settings side car beside it, so the key is wider than it is
     tall). (The wide, nameless pane-layout key was the other; it moved to the tools tier.) See
     Controls/Styles.xaml, section 2.

     Left of the bar is the three temporal split keys; the CENTER is RadarControls (the global time module), held
     on the bar's midpoint by a [*, Auto, *] grid so no amount of growth on either side can shift it; right is the
     app-wide window buttons, all on one spacing. (Until 2026-10-06 the console was left and the keys centred.)
     The gaps between the three clusters are empty bar, by design.

     ⚠️ THE KEYS IN THIS CLUSTER take their LOOK from ONE style chain in Controls/Styles.xaml
     (BarChromeButtonStyle → BarKeyToggleStyle for the ones that latch / BarIconButtonStyle for the one
     that acts): hairline at rest, accent BORDER + raised face when ON, and the SAME SQUARE, set by that
     chain from the shared BarKeySide. ⚠️ The three temporal keys are NOT on that chain — a key divided in
     two cannot use a style whose job is drawing one stroke around one square — so they keep the same
     vocabulary by hand in SplitTemporalToggle. Two homes for one look; both files say so.
     ⚠️ SIZES ARE DECLARED, NOT MEASURED. They used to be: each cluster measured the height its row was
     given and mirrored it onto Width, because the per-pane product chips lived in the bar and changed its
     height. The chips moved to the pane notches and the whole apparatus (a Layout/BarKeyMetrics class, two
     SizeChanged handlers, a name-font probe) was deleted — read the ⚠️ history in Styles.xaml section 2
     before reviving any of it. Nothing here should set a size, corner radius or font per instance unless
     that key is the ONLY consumer of it (the pane key's wide mark, in this file's own Resources) — per-
     instance attributes are what put ~40 duplicated values in this file before.
     The transport buttons in RadarControls are the one cluster still on the old stock look.

     There is no top BAR — the notches are islands, not a strip. A full-width top bar existed once, to hold
     the controls that must not repeat per pane; that distinction now lives inside the one bottom bar, and
     a second full band of chrome was pure cost (a stripe of map covered at the top AND the bottom). A
     notch costs a pane only the few hundred pixels it actually occupies, and hides. -->
<Window
    x:Class="Anvil.MainWindow"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:local="using:Anvil"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    xmlns:controls="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls"
    xmlns:models="using:Anvil.Models"
    xmlns:vm="using:Anvil.ViewModels"
    xmlns:ctl="using:Anvil.Controls.Primitives"
    xmlns:comp="using:Anvil.Controls.Composites"
    mc:Ignorable="d"
    Title="Anvil">

    <!-- Two overlay rows (see the sketch at the top of the file):
           Row 0 — the map band (the map shows through). Every panel that pops over the map is a real
                   top-level OS window owned by WindowManager, so nothing floats in-window.
           Row 1 — the bottom bar, pinned to the bottom edge. Its LEFT is RadarControls (the pane chips +
                   the global time module); its RIGHT is the global cluster below. The map RowSpans both,
                   so the boundary is invisible and costs the map nothing. -->
    <Grid Background="#FF0A0A0A">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>    <!-- map band (the map shows through) -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- bottom bar (tab + bar), pinned to the bottom -->
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- Full-window map (bottom layer; the bar floats on top of it). -->
        <controls:WebView2 x:Name="MainMapWebView" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="2"/>

        <!-- ===== PANE NOTCHES =====
             One per pane: a small hideable island of chrome at the TOP of that pane, holding everything
             that describes THAT pane — its product, its colour-ramp legend, its tilt. It is the only thing
             drawn inside a pane.

               ┌─ pane ──────────────────────────────────────┐
               │              ┌──────────────────┐           │  the notch: centred on the PANE, not on
               │              │ Ref ˅│▓▒░▒▓│0.5°˅│           │  the window, so in multi-pane each one
               │              └─────╮ ˄ ╭───────┘            │  sits over the field it describes
               │                    ╰───╯                    │
               │            …the pane's map…                 │
               └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘

             ⚠️ THE WATERMARK IS GONE. Each pane used to carry a big ghosted product label in its top-left
             corner, because every control lived in the bottom bar and the pane had no other way to say what
             it was showing. The notch names the product a few pixels away, lit, with its ramp attached, so
             the watermark was the same fact said twice and worse. (If a pane ever needs to stay identifiable
             with its notch HIDDEN, that is where a watermark would come back — as the empty state, not as a
             permanent overlay.)

             SAME OVERLAY GRID AS THE WATERMARKS USED: a 3x3 [*, gutter, *] laid over EXACTLY the WebView's
             rect (Row 0 + RowSpan 2, matching the WebView above), so its cells coincide with the page's
             pane rects — both sides split the same area the same way, both using PaneLayoutInfo.GutterPx.
             Each notch is placed by PaneLayoutInfo.CellOf via the x:Bind functions below and self-aligns to
             the TOP CENTRE of its cell.

             ⚠️ The pane ARRANGEMENT (pane 0 = main = bottom-left) is expressed twice: here through CellOf,
             and in map.js paneRects() which lays out the real maps. Same rule, opposite sides of the WebView
             boundary — change both.

             ⚠️ NO IsHitTestVisible="False" on this grid, unlike the watermark grid it replaces: these are
             real controls now. The grid itself has no Background, so its EMPTY areas still hit-test straight
             through to the map — only the notches themselves catch the pointer. Declared BEFORE the bottom
             bar so the bar draws over it and stays clickable where the two overlap.

             ⚠️ Four notches, four INDEPENDENT hide toggles, and no view-model state for any of them: the
             show/hide flag is view state on each OverlayBar. Hiding the notch over the storm you are
             watching while the other three keep their legends is the case that actually comes up. -->
        <!-- ⚠️ THE SITE CHECK TOAST IS GONE from the map band: the announced site check now shows in the bar's
             ACTIVITY SLOT (BarActivityReadout, fed by BarActivityViewModel.WatchSiteCheck). Don't re-add it here. -->

        <Grid x:Name="PaneNotchLayer" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="2">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="{x:Bind PaneGutter}"/> <!-- the groove -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="{x:Bind PaneGutter}"/>   <!-- the groove -->
                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <ctl:OverlayBar x:Name="Notch0" Edge="Top"
                            IsIsland="True"
                            ShowTabLabel="True"
                            EdgeStyle="CastShadow"
                            IsRaised="True"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                            VerticalAlignment="Top"
                            Visibility="{x:Bind NotchVisibility(ViewModel.Radar.Pane0.IsVisible), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.Row="{x:Bind PaneRow(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 0), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.Column="{x:Bind PaneColumn(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 0), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.RowSpan="{x:Bind PaneRowSpan(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 0), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.ColumnSpan="{x:Bind PaneColumnSpan(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 0), Mode=OneWay}">
                <ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
                    <comp:PaneNotchContent Pane="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar.Pane0}"
                                           Radar="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar}"
                                           IsCompact="{x:Bind NotchCompact(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout), Mode=OneWay}"/>
                </ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
            </ctl:OverlayBar>

            <ctl:OverlayBar x:Name="Notch1" Edge="Top"
                            IsIsland="True"
                            ShowTabLabel="True"
                            EdgeStyle="CastShadow"
                            IsRaised="True"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                            VerticalAlignment="Top"
                            Visibility="{x:Bind NotchVisibility(ViewModel.Radar.Pane1.IsVisible), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.Row="{x:Bind PaneRow(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 1), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.Column="{x:Bind PaneColumn(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 1), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.RowSpan="{x:Bind PaneRowSpan(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 1), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.ColumnSpan="{x:Bind PaneColumnSpan(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 1), Mode=OneWay}">
                <ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
                    <comp:PaneNotchContent Pane="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar.Pane1}"
                                           Radar="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar}"
                                           IsCompact="{x:Bind NotchCompact(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout), Mode=OneWay}"/>
                </ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
            </ctl:OverlayBar>

            <ctl:OverlayBar x:Name="Notch2" Edge="Top"
                            IsIsland="True"
                            ShowTabLabel="True"
                            EdgeStyle="CastShadow"
                            IsRaised="True"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                            VerticalAlignment="Top"
                            Visibility="{x:Bind NotchVisibility(ViewModel.Radar.Pane2.IsVisible), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.Row="{x:Bind PaneRow(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 2), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.Column="{x:Bind PaneColumn(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 2), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.RowSpan="{x:Bind PaneRowSpan(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 2), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.ColumnSpan="{x:Bind PaneColumnSpan(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 2), Mode=OneWay}">
                <ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
                    <comp:PaneNotchContent Pane="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar.Pane2}"
                                           Radar="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar}"
                                           IsCompact="{x:Bind NotchCompact(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout), Mode=OneWay}"/>
                </ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
            </ctl:OverlayBar>

            <ctl:OverlayBar x:Name="Notch3" Edge="Top"
                            IsIsland="True"
                            ShowTabLabel="True"
                            EdgeStyle="CastShadow"
                            IsRaised="True"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                            VerticalAlignment="Top"
                            Visibility="{x:Bind NotchVisibility(ViewModel.Radar.Pane3.IsVisible), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.Row="{x:Bind PaneRow(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 3), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.Column="{x:Bind PaneColumn(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 3), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.RowSpan="{x:Bind PaneRowSpan(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 3), Mode=OneWay}"
                            Grid.ColumnSpan="{x:Bind PaneColumnSpan(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout, 3), Mode=OneWay}">
                <ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
                    <comp:PaneNotchContent Pane="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar.Pane3}"
                                           Radar="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar}"
                                           IsCompact="{x:Bind NotchCompact(ViewModel.Radar.PaneLayout), Mode=OneWay}"/>
                </ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
            </ctl:OverlayBar>
        </Grid>

        <!-- LOOP HOLDING GATE — PastCast's loading screen (Composites/LoopHoldingGate). It dims the map band and the
             pane notches and swallows their input while a loop loads; the bottom chrome (row 1, declared AFTER it)
             paints over it and stays live. Transparent and click-through while hidden.
             ⚠️ BOTH ROWS, not row 0: row 1 starts with the shared RAIL, which is see-through apart from its two
             tabs — a row-0 gate left a full-width undimmed strip there (and still would with both tiers hidden).
             Spanning both rows dims under the whole chrome; the opaque tiers cover it, the rail's gaps show it.
             ContentBottomInset (= the opaque tiers' height, MainWindow.UpdateLoopGateInset) keeps the screen's
             content centred on the visible map instead of the whole window. -->
        <comp:LoopHoldingGate x:Name="LoopGate" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="2" ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar.LoopGate}"/>

        <!-- ===== Row 1: the bottom bar. Left = the radar console; CENTER = the temporal mode pills;
             right = the app-wide window buttons. The empty space between them is bar, by design: the
             scrubber stops at the end of the site readout rather than stretching the full width.

             ⚠️ THE PILLS ARE PINNED TO THE BAR'S TRUE HORIZONTAL CENTER and nothing may move them.
             That is why the outer grid is [*, Auto, *] and NOT a content-driven [Auto, Auto, Auto] or a
             pair of stretched halves: two EQUAL star columns split whatever is left over, so the Auto
             column between them lands on the midpoint of the bar no matter how wide either side gets.
             Multi-pane grows the chip cluster on the left (quad = a 2x2 of product combos) — the star
             columns absorb that and the pills do NOT drift. If the left ever grows past its half it
             simply overlaps rather than pushing; the pills are LAST in markup so they stay on top and
             clickable. ===== -->
        <!-- ⚠️ THE BOTTOM CHROME IS TWO STACKED TIERS UNDER ONE SHARED RAIL, bottom-aligned, and its row
             is Auto: the row grows by the tools tier's height, which costs the MAP nothing, because the
             WebView spans both rows at full height either way.
             ⚠️ THE RAIL IS SHARED BECAUSE IT HAS TO BE. An OverlayBar draws its tab as row 0 of its own
             grid, directly above its surface — so the lower tier's tab would land in the SEAM between the
             two tiers, where it can be neither seen nor clicked. Both tiers therefore run ShowTab=False
             and both tabs sit here, above the stack. Hiding a tier shortens the panel and the rail travels
             down with it, still reachable.

             ⚠️⚠️ A GRID, NOT A StackPanel, AND THE RAIL IS DECLARED LAST ON PURPOSE. Both tiers hang their
             cast shadow UPWARD on a negative margin (see OverlayBar), so the tools tier's band reaches up
             into the rail's row. A StackPanel paints in declaration order, which put the tier's band OVER
             the bottom half of both tabs; a Grid lets the rail keep row 0 while being declared last, so it
             paints on top and the shadow passes behind it. ⚠️ Keep the rail last in markup — moving it
             back up to read in stacking order is the one edit that silently re-breaks this. -->
        <Grid x:Name="BottomChrome" Grid.Row="1" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" SizeChanged="OnBottomChromeSizeChanged">
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- the shared rail (declared LAST, see above) -->
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- upper tier: the map tools -->
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- the bar -->
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <!-- The UPPER TIER: the map's camera tools, on the raised surface. Its visibility is PERSISTED
                 (AppSettings.MapControlsStripVisible) — unlike the bar's, which is pure view state — so the
                 Tools tab two-ways the view-model flag rather than this control's own. -->
            <ctl:OverlayBar x:Name="MapControlsTier"
                            Grid.Row="1"
                            ShowTab="False"
                            IsRaised="True"
                            EdgeStyle="CastShadow"
                            IsOverlayBarVisible="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsMapControlsStripVisible, Mode=OneWay}">
                <ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
                    <comp:MapControlsStrip x:Name="ToolsStrip" ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel}"/>
                </ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
            </ctl:OverlayBar>

            <!-- ⚠️ EdgeStyle IS DYNAMIC HERE, and it is the only place in the app that it moves.
                 With the tools tier showing, the bar RECEIVES that tier's shadow. Hide the tier and the
                 bar becomes the top of the assembly, with the map above it - so it has to start CASTING
                 instead. MainWindow.ApplyRailSeating owns the swap; a fixed value is wrong in one of
                 the two states whichever one you pick. -->
            <ctl:OverlayBar x:Name="BottomBar" Grid.Row="2" ShowTab="False" EdgeStyle="InsetShadow">
                <ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
                    <Grid x:Name="BarGrid" ColumnSpacing="16">
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>    <!-- temporal keys, left-aligned  ┐ equal stars: -->
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- radar console, DEAD CENTER   │ the Auto     -->
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>    <!-- window buttons, right edge   ┘ sits centered-->
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                        <!-- ===== CENTERPIECE: the time module, alone =====
                             Everything here is about READING the radar that is loaded, in time.
                             ⚠️ SWAPPED WITH THE TEMPORAL KEYS (2026-10-06, the user's mock-up A): the console was the
                             LEFT cluster and the keys held the midpoint. Now the bar reads mode → time → windows. The
                             tools tier's centre section spans its edges, picker on play (MainWindow.AlignCentreSection), and
                             the activity slot runs from its right edge to Atlas.
                             ⚠️ THE PLAY BUTTON IS THE BAR'S MIDPOINT, not the module's middle: RadarControls pads its
                             short side (CentreOnPlay) so its centre IS play's.
                             ⚠️ CENTRED ONLY BECAUSE IT IS A FIXED WIDTH: the clock reserves a two-digit hour
                             (RadarControls), so 9:59 → 10:00 can't re-centre the module and slide the scrubber.
                             ⚠️ INSPECT, the markers key and the Home key all left for the tools tier earlier
                             (MapControlsStrip). Don't re-add any here. -->
                        <comp:RadarControls x:Name="TimeModule"
                                            Grid.Column="1"
                                            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                            VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                            ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar}"/>

                        <!-- ===== GLOBAL CLUSTER =====
                             Every key here shares the bar's one look with the temporal keys at the centre:
                             hairline at rest, accent border + raised face when ON. Nothing here repeats per
                             pane.

                             ONE KIND of control sits here: Atlas and Settings are INDEPENDENT window latches (any
                             combination open). (The pane-layout key and Location used to sit here too; both moved
                             to the tools tier — see MapControlsStrip.) ⚠️ They are NOT
                             separated by a wider gap any more: this was two nested grids at 14px and 6px, which
                             read as a spacing mistake rather than as a grouping. ONE spacing for all of them,
                             matching the temporal cluster's, so the whole bar has a single rhythm. Keep it one
                             flat grid — the moment it nests again the gaps drift apart. -->
                        <Grid x:Name="RightCluster"
                              Grid.Column="2"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              ColumnSpacing="8">
                            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- Radar Atlas -->
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- settings -->
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- window caption (min / max / close) -->
                            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                            <!-- ⚠️ THE PANE-LAYOUT KEY AND THE LOCATION KEY ARE GONE from this cluster — both
                                 moved to the tools tier (MapControlsStrip): pane layout as three 1/2/4 toggles
                                 after Fit to view, Location beside the place search. Don't re-add them here. -->

                            <!-- ===== WINDOW BUTTONS =====
                                 Each latches while its window is open (IsChecked two-ways with the open flag),
                                 and any combination may be open at once.

                                 ⚠️ GLYPH OVER A NAME, same as the temporal keys — these were glyph-only, and a
                                 bar where the centre keys are named and the right ones are not made the right
                                 cluster read as decoration. A glyph alone also has to be LEARNED: a globe, a
                                 tower and a gear are only obvious once you already know what is behind them,
                                 and the tooltip that carried that meaning needs a hover to appear.

                                 ⚠️ The names cost NO width: a key is a fixed SQUARE, so the name is fitted
                                 INSIDE the square it already occupied (BarKeyNameSize, Styles.xaml). The
                                 cluster is exactly as wide as it was, and the centred temporal keys do not
                                 move. Keep it that way — a name long enough to force a wider key breaks both,
                                 and there is no probe measuring it for you any more.

                                 ⚠️ The style is BarKeyToggleStyle, NOT the old BarIconToggleStyle (deleted):
                                 that one set the icon FontFamily on the BUTTON, which now renders the NAME in
                                 the icon font — boxes instead of words. The glyph carries its own font on the
                                 FontIcon. -->
                            <!-- ⚠️ THERE WERE FOUR KEYS HERE. "Map" (global map controls) and the
                                 Debug-only "Dev" key are gone: their windows became TABS of the Settings
                                 window, so the gear now opens all of it. Adding a group of settings means
                                 adding a tab (Controls/Windows/SettingsWindow), NOT a key here — this
                                 cluster is for windows that are not settings.
                                 ⚠️ The Timeframe key briefly lived here too, as the third window latch. It
                                 moved to the CENTRE cluster, hard against the temporal trio, because a
                                 panel that only ever configures those three modes belongs beside them —
                                 see TemporalToggles. What is left here is genuinely global. -->
                                <!-- ⚠️ ATLAS WAS A SPLIT KEY (a SplitTemporalToggle whose car opened a home/favorites
                                     flyout). That flyout became the tools tier's SITE PICKER (MapControlsStrip), so this
                                     is a plain window latch again, exactly like Settings. Don't re-split it.
                                     ⚠️ Its glyph (EC05) is MIRRORED on PastCastTab's Timeframe-card "Atlas" button — same
                                     door, same mark; change both.
                                     ⚠️ ONE-WAY + Click → ToggleAtlasFromBar: this key always opens on RADAR SITES (the
                                     user's call, 2026-10-01; PastCast's button opens on Past events). -->
                                <ToggleButton x:Name="AtlasKey"
                                              Grid.Column="0"
                                              Style="{StaticResource BarKeyToggleStyle}"
                                              ToolTipService.ToolTip="Anvil Atlas — radar sites and past events"
                                              IsChecked="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsRadarAtlasOpen, Mode=OneWay}"
                                              Click="OnAtlasKeyClick">
                                    <Grid Margin="3,0" RowSpacing="2">
                                        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                                        </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                                        <FontIcon Grid.Row="0"
                                                  FontFamily="Segoe Fluent Icons,Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                                                  FontSize="{StaticResource BarKeyGlyphSize}"
                                                  Glyph="&#xEC05;"/>
                                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1"
                                                   FontSize="{StaticResource BarKeyNameSize}"
                                                   Text="Atlas"
                                                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"/>
                                    </Grid>
                                </ToggleButton>
                                <!-- ⚠️ THE "Map" KEY IS GONE (it was column 3, glyph E774, latching
                                     IsMapControlsStripVisible). The map-tools tier now carries its own tab on
                                     the rail above the bar, which is the same gesture in the place the thing
                                     actually is — so the key was a second control for one flag, and the one
                                     further from what it toggles. The FLAG is untouched and still persisted;
                                     the tab two-ways it. Don't re-add a key for it. -->
                                <ToggleButton x:Name="SettingsKey"
                                              Grid.Column="1"
                                              Style="{StaticResource BarKeyToggleStyle}"
                                              ToolTipService.ToolTip="Settings — map, radar, storage"
                                              IsChecked="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsSettingsWindowOpen, Mode=TwoWay}">
                                    <Grid Margin="3,0" RowSpacing="2">
                                        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                                        </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                                        <FontIcon x:Name="SettingsGlyph" Grid.Row="0"
                                                  FontFamily="Segoe Fluent Icons,Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                                                  FontSize="{StaticResource BarKeyGlyphSize}"
                                                  Glyph="&#xE713;"/>
                                        <TextBlock x:Name="SettingsName" Grid.Row="1"
                                                   FontSize="{StaticResource BarKeyNameSize}"
                                                   Text="Settings"
                                                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"/>
                                    </Grid>
                                </ToggleButton>
                                <!-- THE WINDOW'S OWN CAPTION BUTTONS, moved here: the system min/max/close are hidden
                                     (the right-edge ForeCast window covered them) — see the CAPTION BAND block in
                                     MainWindow.xaml.cs. Last in the cluster, so it is the bar's far-right key.
                                     ⚠️ SET APART: 42 + the grid's 8 = 50 px from Settings, so the window's own buttons
                                     don't read as a third app key (the user's call, 2026-09-30). The tools tier's
                                     isolation picker re-measures off Atlas, so changing this gap needs nothing else. -->
                                <ctl:WindowCaptionKey x:Name="CaptionKey"
                                                      Grid.Column="2"
                                                      Margin="42,0,0,0"
                                                      MinimizeRequested="OnCaptionMinimize"
                                                      MaximizeRestoreRequested="OnCaptionMaximizeRestore"
                                                      CloseRequested="OnCaptionClose"/>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- ===== ACTIVITY SLOT — what Anvil is working on (Composites/BarActivityReadout) =====
                             The empty bar between the console and Atlas. Its span is MEASURED, not laid out:
                             spans all three columns, Left-aligned, and AlignActivityBay sets its left margin to the
                             console's right edge + 24 and its width to Atlas's left edge less the cluster's 8 px.
                             Declared BEFORE the
                             temporal keys so they stay on top. Nothing active = the plate fades out. -->
                        <comp:BarActivityReadout x:Name="ActivityBay"
                                                 Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
                                                 HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                 Width="0"
                                                 ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel.Activity}"/>

                        <!-- ===== TEMPORAL MODE KEYS — the bar's LEFT edge =====
                             They held the midpoint until 2026-10-06 (the console took it). LAST in markup on
                             purpose: in a window narrow enough that the centred console reaches into this column,
                             the console slides UNDER these rather than over them, so the mode keys stay clickable.
                             Past excludes Now/Fore; Now and Fore coexist.
                             ⚠️ VerticalAlignment Center, not Stretch: the keys are a fixed size (Styles.xaml,
                             BarKeySide) and this row is what makes the BAR that tall. -->
                        <comp:TemporalToggles x:Name="TemporalKeys"
                                              Grid.Column="0"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                              ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel}"/>
                    </Grid>
                </ctl:OverlayBar.BarContent>
            </ctl:OverlayBar>

            <!-- ⚠️ THE RAIL IS ROW 0 BUT DECLARED LAST — see the ⚠⚠ at the top of this Grid. It has to
                 paint AFTER the tools tier, because that tier's cast shadow hangs up into this row and
                 would otherwise darken the bottom half of both tabs.
                 ⚠️ BOTH TABS WEAR THE PLATE THEY STAND ON, never the tier they toggle, so their face MOVES
                 with the stack: raised while the map-tools tier is up, ground once it is hidden and the
                 rail comes to rest on the bar. Set in MainWindow.ApplyRailSeating, which owns that rule
                 together with the bar's EdgeStyle — they answer the same question and drifting apart
                 would light the tabs for one plate while the shadow describes another.
                 ⚠️ They used to be keyed to the tier each one TOGGLES (Tools raised, Bar ground) so the
                 colours told them apart; that made the Bar tab a darker notch in a raised edge, a tab on a
                 surface it did not match. The LABELS disambiguate — which is why they are NOUNS, since two
                 lozenges both reading "Hide" name no difference between themselves.
                 ⚠️ ORDER MATCHES THE STACK: Tools (the upper tier) left, Bar (the lower) right. -->
            <StackPanel Grid.Row="0"
                        Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Spacing="10">
                <ctl:OverlayBarTab x:Name="ToolsTab" Target="Tools"
                                   IsShown="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsMapControlsStripVisible, Mode=TwoWay}"/>
                <ctl:OverlayBarTab x:Name="BarTab" Target="Bar"
                                   IsShown="{x:Bind BottomBar.IsOverlayBarVisible, Mode=TwoWay}"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
    </Grid>
</Window>
